KNOWNBUG
main.cpp

^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ function-typed parameter: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [dcl.fct]/5: a parameter of function type is adjusted to a pointer to
function, so `typedef int F(int());' declares F as int(int (*)()), the type
of cb.  The front end takes the parameter `int()' as int: `F *p = cb;' is a
conversion error ("invalid implicit conversion from 'auto (*)(auto (*)() ->
signed int) -> signed int' to 'auto (*)(signed int) -> signed int'").  Not
yet diagnosed.
Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and clang++ 18 accept this program, and it
fails only its WRONG assertion.
